// What a reader means by a character, and the tables that answer it: case
// mapping, grapheme clusters and display width.
//
// **Unicode 16.0.0.** Every table below is generated from the Unicode
// Character Database, and the command that generated it is quoted above it.
// `unicode.VERSION` is the version those files were, so a program can print
// what it was built against.
//
// | | |
// |---|---|
// | `upper`, `lower`, `fold` | full case mapping, and a key for caseless comparison |
// | `graphemes`, `grapheme_count` | UAX #29: what a reader counts as one character |
// | `width` | how many terminal columns text takes |
//
// Three counts, each named for what it counts: `s.size()` is bytes,
// `s.chars().size()` is code points, `unicode.grapheme_count(s)` is
// characters as a reader means the word.
//
// --- why this is a module and not a method ---------------------------------
//
// `s.to_upper()` and `s.to_lower()` stay **ASCII only**: `"école".to_upper()`
// is `"éCOLE"`. This module's `unicode.upper(s)` is `"ÉCOLE"`. Both exist,
// and the division is deliberate:
//
//   - A method on every `str` is a promise the language keeps everywhere,
//     and the language is one binary with no data files. Full case mapping
//     is ~28 KB of tables; putting it behind a method would put it in every
//     program that uppercases an option name.
//   - The ASCII rule is total and needs nothing: it is right for a protocol
//     token, a header name, a file extension, a hex digit -- which is most
//     of what a program uppercases, and all of what the standard library
//     does. It is also the one mapping that is the same in every language.
//   - Full case mapping is not a function from one character to one
//     character (`ß` is `SS`, one code point becoming two), it depends on
//     the rest of the string (a final sigma), and in three languages it
//     depends on the human language the text is in. None of that belongs
//     behind a name as innocent as `to_upper`.
//
// So: `str` answers the ASCII question, `unicode` answers the Unicode one,
// and a reader of a call site can tell which was meant. Importing this
// module is what costs the tables -- a program that does not import it emits
// none of them, because a module's constants are emitted only when the
// module is loaded.
//
// --- no locale ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `SpecialCasing.txt` has sixteen conditional mappings. One is conditional on
// the text alone -- the Greek final sigma, `Σ` at the end of a word lowercases
// to `ς`, not `σ` -- and this module implements it, because the condition is
// in the string and an implementation that ignored it would be wrong about
// ordinary Greek. The other fifteen are conditional on a **language**:
// Turkish and Azeri map `I` to `ı` and `i` to `İ`, and Lithuanian keeps a dot
// above a lowercase i under an accent.
//
// Those are not implemented, and `upper`/`lower` take no locale, because a
// locale is not a property of a `str`. The language has no locale type, no
// notion of a current one, and a `str` carries no tag saying what language
// its text is in -- so the only honest spelling would be an extra argument
// on every call, which every call site would have to answer, almost always
// with "none". A program that genuinely needs Turkish casing knows it does,
// and can map `I` and `i` itself before calling: the two code points are
// spelled out, in the open, in that program, rather than hidden behind a
// flag that is wrong by default. Getting this wrong silently is the famous
// bug (`"I".to_lower() == "i"` is false in Turkish), and it is worse when a
// library guesses the locale from the environment, as C's `tolower` does.
//
// Turkic **case folding** (`CaseFolding.txt` status T) is dropped for the
// same reason: `fold` is the default, language-independent folding, which is
// what a caseless comparison of text of unknown origin wants.
//
// --- the shape of the tables ------------------------------------------------
//
// Simple mapping is a list of runs, `(lo, hi, step, delta)`: every code point
// from `lo` to `hi`, `step` apart, maps by adding `delta`. The step is what
// makes it small -- Latin Extended-A is 256 alternating upper/lower pairs,
// `0100 0102 0104 ...` all mapping by -1, which is one run with a step of 2
// rather than 128 runs. 1,477 upper mappings come to 205 runs, and a lookup
// is a binary search over them.
//
// A mapping that is not one-to-one -- `ß` to `SS` -- cannot be a delta, so it
// is a second, smaller table looked up first: sorted keys, and the values
// flattened into one array with an offset per key.

/// The version of the Unicode Character Database these tables come from.
pub const str VERSION = "16.0.0";

/// `s` with every character replaced by its uppercase form.
///
/// Full mapping, so the result may be longer than the input in characters as
/// well as in bytes: `unicode.upper("straße")` is `"STRASSE"`. Not
/// locale-sensitive; see the module header.
pub str upper(str s) {
    List<int> out = [];
    for (int c in s.chars()) {
        int at = key_index(SPECIAL_UPPER_FROM, c);
        if (at >= 0) {
            append_slice(out, SPECIAL_UPPER_TO, SPECIAL_UPPER_AT[at], SPECIAL_UPPER_AT[at + 1]);
        } else {
            out.push(c + run_delta(UPPER_RUNS, c));
        }
    }
    return str.from_chars(out);
}

/// `s` with every character replaced by its lowercase form.
///
/// A capital sigma at the end of a word becomes `ς` rather than `σ`, which
/// is the one context-dependent rule this module implements (see the header);
/// `unicode.lower("ΟΔΟΣ")` is `"οδος"`.
pub str lower(str s) {
    List<int> cps = s.chars();
    List<int> out = [];
    int n = cps.size();
    int i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        int c = cps[i];
        if (c == CAPITAL_SIGMA && final_sigma(cps, i)) {
            out.push(FINAL_SIGMA);
        } else {
            int at = key_index(SPECIAL_LOWER_FROM, c);
            if (at >= 0) {
                append_slice(out, SPECIAL_LOWER_TO, SPECIAL_LOWER_AT[at], SPECIAL_LOWER_AT[at + 1]);
            } else {
                out.push(c + run_delta(LOWER_RUNS, c));
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return str.from_chars(out);
}

/// `s` folded for caseless comparison: `unicode.fold(a) == unicode.fold(b)`
/// is the caseless equality of `a` and `b`.
///
/// This is Unicode's full, default case folding (`CaseFolding.txt`, statuses
/// C and F), which is *not* the same thing as lowercasing: it maps `ß` to
/// `ss` and `ς` to `σ` so that the pairs compare equal, and it is defined to
/// be stable under itself. The result is not text to show anyone -- it is a
/// key. Folding does not normalise, so `"é"` and `"e\u{301}"` still differ;
/// that is `nfc` first (§NFC), and neither is a collation.
pub str fold(str s) {
    List<int> out = [];
    for (int c in s.chars()) {
        int at = key_index(FOLD_FULL_FROM, c);
        if (at >= 0) {
            append_slice(out, FOLD_FULL_TO, FOLD_FULL_AT[at], FOLD_FULL_AT[at + 1]);
        } else {
            out.push(c + run_delta(FOLD_RUNS, c));
        }
    }
    return str.from_chars(out);
}

// --- the lookups -------------------------------------------------------------

/// What to add to `c` to map it, or 0 if this table leaves it alone.
///
/// A binary search over `(lo, hi, step, delta)` runs of four. A table is a
/// module constant, so it is static data in the emitted program and this is
/// four bounds-checked loads a step.
int run_delta(Array<int> runs, int c) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = runs.size() / 4 - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        int at = mid * 4;
        if (c < runs[at]) {
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else if (c > runs[at + 1]) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else if ((c - runs[at]) % runs[at + 2] == 0) {
            return runs[at + 3];
        } else {
            // Inside a run, but on one of the code points it steps over.
            return 0;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

/// Where `c` is in a sorted array of keys, or -1.
int key_index(Array<int> keys, int c) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = keys.size() - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        if (c < keys[mid]) {
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else if (c > keys[mid]) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            return mid;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

/// Append `from[at .. end]` to `out`.
void append_slice(List<int> out, Array<int> from, int at, int end) {
    int i = at;
    while (i < end) {
        out.push(from[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

/// Is `c` in a sorted list of `(lo, hi)` ranges?
bool in_ranges(Array<int> ranges, int c) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = ranges.size() / 2 - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        int at = mid * 2;
        if (c < ranges[at]) {
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else if (c > ranges[at + 1]) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// --- the final sigma ----------------------------------------------------------

/// U+03A3 GREEK CAPITAL LETTER SIGMA.
const int CAPITAL_SIGMA = 931;
/// U+03C2 GREEK SMALL LETTER FINAL SIGMA.
const int FINAL_SIGMA = 962;

/// Is the sigma at `cps[i]` at the end of a word?
///
/// UAX #21's `Final_Sigma` condition, word for word: it is preceded by a
/// cased character, and not followed by one -- looking through characters
/// that carry no case of their own (accents, the apostrophe in `ΜΑΪΟΣ'`) in
/// both directions. `"ΣΣ"` lowercases to `"σς"`: the first sigma has nothing
/// cased before it, the second has nothing cased after it.
bool final_sigma(List<int> cps, int i) {
    int j = i - 1;
    while (j >= 0 && in_ranges(CASE_IGNORABLE, cps[j])) {
        j = j - 1;
    }
    if (j < 0 || !in_ranges(CASED, cps[j])) {
        return false;
    }
    int n = cps.size();
    int k = i + 1;
    while (k < n && in_ranges(CASE_IGNORABLE, cps[k])) {
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return k >= n || !in_ranges(CASED, cps[k]);
}

// --- grapheme clusters ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// UAX #29's extended grapheme clusters: what a reader calls one character,
// and what a text editor moves the cursor over. `size()` counts bytes,
// `chars().size()` counts code points, `unicode.grapheme_count(s)` counts
// these -- three counts, each named for what it counts.
//
// The rules are a small state machine over a class per code point, and the
// class comes from GCB_RANGES. Everything with a name below is a rule of
// UAX #29 §3.1.1, quoted where it is checked. The state the machine carries
// is three counters, because three rules look further back than one
// character: a run of regional indicators (a flag is two, so the third one
// starts a new flag), an emoji joined by ZWJ, and an Indic consonant
// conjunct.

/// The grapheme clusters of `s`, in order: one element per character a
/// reader would count. `unicode.graphemes("")` is empty, and joining the
/// result with `""` gives `s` back.
pub List<str> graphemes(str s) {
    List<int> starts = cluster_starts(s);
    List<str> out = [];
    int i = 1;
    while (i < starts.size()) {
        out.push(s.substr(starts[i - 1], starts[i]));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

/// How many grapheme clusters `s` has, without building them.
///
/// `"👍🏽".size()` is 8, `"👍🏽".chars().size()` is 2, and this is 1.
pub int grapheme_count(str s) {
    return cluster_starts(s).size() - 1;
}

/// The byte offset where each cluster begins, and `s.size()` last, so that
/// consecutive offsets bracket a cluster. Empty text gives `[0]`, one
/// offset and no clusters.
///
/// Offsets rather than strings because both callers want them: `graphemes`
/// cuts the original bytes out with `substr` (which is why nothing is
/// re-encoded, and why every offset is on a character boundary by
/// construction), and `width` only measures.
List<int> cluster_starts(str s) {
    List<int> cps = s.chars();
    List<int> out = [0];
    int n = cps.size();
    int at = 0;
    int prev = 0;
    // The three pieces of history the rules need. `ri` is how many regional
    // indicators run up to the character before the one being judged; `pic`
    // is 1 after an emoji and any extending characters, 2 once a ZWJ has
    // followed it; `cons` is 1 after an Indic consonant and any extenders,
    // 2 once one of them was a linker.
    int ri = 0;
    int pic = 0;
    int cons = 0;
    int i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        int cur = class_of(cps[i]);
        if (i > 0) {
            at = at + utf8_len(cps[i - 1]);
            if (breaks(prev, cur, ri, pic, cons)) {
                out.push(at);
            }
        }
        int g = cur & 15;
        if (g == GCB_REGIONAL_INDICATOR) {
            ri = ri + 1;
        } else {
            ri = 0;
        }
        if (cur & 16 != 0) {
            pic = 1;
        } else if (pic == 1 && g == GCB_EXTEND) {
            pic = 1;
        } else if (pic == 1 && g == GCB_ZWJ) {
            pic = 2;
        } else {
            pic = 0;
        }
        int b = cur >> 5 & 3;
        if (b == INCB_CONSONANT) {
            cons = 1;
        } else if (cons > 0 && b == INCB_LINKER) {
            cons = 2;
        } else if (cons > 0 && b == INCB_EXTEND) {
            cons = cons;
        } else {
            cons = 0;
        }
        prev = cur;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (n > 0) {
        out.push(s.size());
    }
    return out;
}

/// Is there a cluster boundary between `prev` and `cur`?
///
/// The rules of UAX #29 §3.1.1 in their order, which matters: the first one
/// that matches decides. GB1, GB2 (start and end of text) are the caller's,
/// and GB999 -- break anywhere else -- is the last line.
bool breaks(int prev, int cur, int ri, int pic, int cons) {
    int p = prev & 15;
    int c = cur & 15;
    // GB3: CR LF is one cluster, and GB4/GB5: everything else parts around
    // a control character, in both directions.
    if (p == GCB_CR && c == GCB_LF) {
        return false;
    }
    if (p == GCB_CR || p == GCB_LF || p == GCB_CONTROL) {
        return true;
    }
    if (c == GCB_CR || c == GCB_LF || c == GCB_CONTROL) {
        return true;
    }
    // GB6, GB7, GB8: a Hangul syllable spelled out in jamo stays together.
    if (p == GCB_L && (c == GCB_L || c == GCB_V || c == GCB_LV || c == GCB_LVT)) {
        return false;
    }
    if ((p == GCB_LV || p == GCB_V) && (c == GCB_V || c == GCB_T)) {
        return false;
    }
    if ((p == GCB_LVT || p == GCB_T) && c == GCB_T) {
        return false;
    }
    // GB9, GB9a, GB9b: an accent, a spacing mark or a joiner belongs to what
    // it is attached to, and a prepending character to what follows it.
    if (c == GCB_EXTEND || c == GCB_ZWJ) {
        return false;
    }
    if (c == GCB_SPACINGMARK) {
        return false;
    }
    if (p == GCB_PREPEND) {
        return false;
    }
    // GB9c: an Indic conjunct -- consonant, linker, consonant -- is one
    // cluster, however many extending characters are in between.
    if (cons == 2 && cur >> 5 & 3 == INCB_CONSONANT) {
        return false;
    }
    // GB11: emoji ZWJ emoji, which is how a family is spelled.
    if (pic == 2 && cur & 16 != 0) {
        return false;
    }
    // GB12, GB13: regional indicators pair up into flags, so a break falls
    // between pairs and not inside one.
    if (p == GCB_REGIONAL_INDICATOR && c == GCB_REGIONAL_INDICATOR && ri % 2 == 1) {
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// The packed class of `c`: a Grapheme_Cluster_Break value in the low four
/// bits, Extended_Pictographic in bit 4, and Indic_Conjunct_Break in bits
/// 5 and 6.
int class_of(int c) {
    // The 11,172 Hangul syllables are arithmetic, not a table: they are laid
    // out as (lead, vowel, trailing), and a syllable with no trailing jamo is
    // LV, one with is LVT. That is 798 lines of GraphemeBreakProperty.txt
    // this table does not carry.
    if (c >= HANGUL_BASE && c < HANGUL_BASE + HANGUL_COUNT) {
        if ((c - HANGUL_BASE) % HANGUL_TRAILING == 0) {
            return GCB_LV;
        }
        return GCB_LVT;
    }
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = GCB_RANGES.size() / 3 - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        int at = mid * 3;
        if (c < GCB_RANGES[at]) {
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else if (c > GCB_RANGES[at + 1]) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            return GCB_RANGES[at + 2];
        }
    }
    return GCB_OTHER;
}

/// How many bytes `c` takes in UTF-8. The four cases of §3.2a, in the same
/// shape `__text.from_chars` encodes them.
int utf8_len(int c) {
    if (c < 128) {
        return 1;
    }
    if (c < 2048) {
        return 2;
    }
    if (c < 65536) {
        return 3;
    }
    return 4;
}

// The Grapheme_Cluster_Break values, in the order GCB_RANGES was generated
// with: the low four bits of a packed class.
const int GCB_OTHER = 0;
const int GCB_CR = 1;
const int GCB_LF = 2;
const int GCB_CONTROL = 3;
const int GCB_EXTEND = 4;
const int GCB_ZWJ = 5;
const int GCB_REGIONAL_INDICATOR = 6;
const int GCB_PREPEND = 7;
const int GCB_SPACINGMARK = 8;
const int GCB_L = 9;
const int GCB_V = 10;
const int GCB_T = 11;
const int GCB_LV = 12;
const int GCB_LVT = 13;
// Indic_Conjunct_Break, in bits 5 and 6.
const int INCB_NONE = 0;
const int INCB_LINKER = 1;
const int INCB_CONSONANT = 2;
const int INCB_EXTEND = 3;
/// U+AC00, the first Hangul syllable, and the shape of the block: 19 leads
/// times 21 vowels times 28 trailings (the first of which is "none").
const int HANGUL_BASE = 44032;
const int HANGUL_TRAILING = 28;
const int HANGUL_COUNT = 11172;

// --- display width --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// How many columns `s` takes in a terminal that draws text in a grid: an
// East Asian wide or fullwidth character is two, a combining mark is none,
// everything else is one. This is what a table of options, a progress bar or
// a box drawing has to know, and `size()` is the wrong answer for all three
// -- `args` pads its help table by `size()` today, so a non-ASCII option name
// misaligns its column; that is for whoever changes `args`, not this module.
//
// **Width is per grapheme cluster, not per code point.** `👍🏽` is two code
// points, each of them wide, and a terminal draws one glyph two columns
// wide; summing per code point would say four. So the text is clustered
// first (UAX #29, above) and each cluster is measured once, by its base
// character.
//
// There is no standard that says what a terminal does -- EastAsianWidth.txt
// is a property, not a promise -- and terminals disagree, most visibly about
// emoji. The rules here are the ones the common terminals agree on:
//
//   - a cluster whose base is East Asian Wide or Fullwidth: 2 columns;
//   - a cluster carrying U+FE0F, the emoji presentation selector: 2, which
//     is what makes `1️⃣` two columns although `1` is one;
//   - a pair of regional indicators -- a flag: 2, although each half is
//     neutral on its own;
//   - a cluster whose base is a combining mark, a format character or a
//     control: 0;
//   - everything else: 1.
//
// A control character is 0 rather than an error: this answers "how wide is
// this text", and a program that cares whether its text has control characters
// asks that separately.

/// The width of `s` in terminal columns.
pub int width(str s) {
    List<int> starts = cluster_starts(s);
    int total = 0;
    int i = 1;
    while (i < starts.size()) {
        total = total + cluster_width(s.substr(starts[i - 1], starts[i]));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return total;
}

/// The width of one cluster, by the rules in the section comment.
int cluster_width(str cluster) {
    List<int> cps = cluster.chars();
    if (cps.size() == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    int ri = 0;
    for (int c in cps) {
        if (c == EMOJI_PRESENTATION) {
            return 2;
        }
        if (c >= REGIONAL_INDICATOR_FIRST && c <= REGIONAL_INDICATOR_LAST) {
            ri = ri + 1;
        }
    }
    if (ri == 2) {
        return 2;
    }
    return char_width(cps[0]);
}

/// The width of one code point: 0, 1 or 2, from WIDTH_RANGES, which holds
/// only the code points that are not 1.
int char_width(int c) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = WIDTH_RANGES.size() / 3 - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        int at = mid * 3;
        if (c < WIDTH_RANGES[at]) {
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else if (c > WIDTH_RANGES[at + 1]) {
            lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            return WIDTH_RANGES[at + 2];
        }
    }
    return 1;
}

/// U+FE0F VARIATION SELECTOR-16, which asks for the emoji form.
const int EMOJI_PRESENTATION = 65039;
/// U+1F1E6 and U+1F1FF, the letters a flag is spelled with.
const int REGIONAL_INDICATOR_FIRST = 127462;
const int REGIONAL_INDICATOR_LAST = 127487;

// --- the tables ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Generated, not written. Every array below came out of
//
//     python3 gen.py case
//
// reading the Unicode Character Database 16.0.0 as installed at
// /usr/share/unicode/ucd. The script is not in the repository (it is a
// build-time tool for a file that changes once a Unicode version); what it
// does to each table is written above that table, and the counts are its
// output, so a regeneration that disagrees with them is a change to report.
//
// The run encoder both simple tables use, and the whole of the compression:
// a code point maps by adding `delta`, runs of code points that share one
// delta become one entry, and a run whose members are two apart -- the
// alternating upper/lower pairs Latin Extended-A and Cyrillic are built from
// -- becomes one entry with a step of 2 rather than one entry each.
//
//     def runs_of(m):                    # m: cp -> mapped cp, from the UCD
//         cps = sorted(m); out = []; i = 0
//         while i < len(cps):
//             cp = cps[i]; d = m[cp] - cp; j = i; k = i
//             while (j + 1 < len(cps) and cps[j+1] == cps[j] + 1
//                    and m[cps[j+1]] - cps[j+1] == d): j += 1
//             while (k + 1 < len(cps) and cps[k+1] == cps[k] + 2
//                    and m[cps[k+1]] - cps[k+1] == d): k += 1
//             if k > j: out.append([cp, cps[k], 2, d]); i = k + 1
//             else:     out.append([cp, cps[j], 1, d]); i = j + 1
//         return out

// Simple uppercase: UnicodeData.txt field 12, 1477 mappings in 205 runs of
// (lo, hi, step, delta).
const Array<int> UPPER_RUNS = [
    97, 122, 1, -32, 181, 181, 1, 743, 224, 246, 1, -32,
    248, 254, 1, -32, 255, 255, 1, 121, 257, 303, 2, -1,
    305, 305, 1, -232, 307, 311, 2, -1, 314, 328, 2, -1,
    331, 375, 2, -1, 378, 382, 2, -1, 383, 383, 1, -300,
    384, 384, 1, 195, 387, 389, 2, -1, 392, 392, 1, -1,
    396, 396, 1, -1, 402, 402, 1, -1, 405, 405, 1, 97,
    409, 409, 1, -1, 410, 410, 1, 163, 411, 411, 1, 42561,
    414, 414, 1, 130, 417, 421, 2, -1, 424, 424, 1, -1,
    429, 429, 1, -1, 432, 432, 1, -1, 436, 438, 2, -1,
    441, 441, 1, -1, 445, 445, 1, -1, 447, 447, 1, 56,
